# Coding Standards

## General

Write production-quality code.

Optimize for

- Readability
- Maintainability
- Scalability

---

## Principles

Follow

- SOLID
- DRY
- KISS

---

## Functions

- Small
- Single responsibility
- Descriptive names
- Avoid deep nesting

---

## Variables

Use meaningful names.

Avoid abbreviations.

Good

customer_name

Bad

cn

---

## Comments

Write comments only when needed.

Prefer self-explanatory code.

---

## Imports

Organize imports.

Remove unused imports.

---

## Error Handling

Handle expected errors.

Do not silently ignore exceptions.

---

## Security

Validate all inputs.

Never trust client data.

Never hardcode

- passwords
- API keys
- secrets

---

## Refactoring

Do not refactor unrelated code.

Preserve existing behavior.

---

## Before Coding

Always

1. Explain the solution.
2. Mention assumptions.
3. List affected files.
4. Then generate code.

---

## Quality Checklist

Before finishing

- No syntax errors
- Correct imports
- Type hints
- No duplicated code
- No placeholder implementations
- Consistent formatting

---
alwaysApply: true
---

## Environment Files

Environment files are part of the project.

Files

- backend/.env
- backend/.env.production
- frontend/.env
- frontend/.env.production

These files must be maintained whenever configuration changes.

Do not remove them.

Do not rename them.

If a new environment variable is introduced, update both development and production environment files.